  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Outside the hotel, the real Iosif Prigozhin—now without the prop moustache—spots Max and Vika, proving beyond doubt that Max was telling the truth. Moved by Max's honesty, Vika apologizes and shares the happy news that they are having a baby girl.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Привет, Иосиф! Извини.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Что это у тебя?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "На тебя хотел быть похожим.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Лучше бы здесь отрастил.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Ой, а вы правда Иосиф Пригожин?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "И слава богу!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Да ладно!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Прости, что не поверила.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "На твоём месте я бы тоже не поверил.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "У нас будет девочка.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Правда? Девочка?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я так и знал!",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Привет, Иосиф! Извини.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hello, Iosif! Sorry.' The meaning hinges on 'hello' working together with 'sorry' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Что это у тебя?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What is that on you?' The grammar packs 'what' and 'you' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "На тебя хотел быть похожим.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I wanted to look like you.' Max anchors the line on 'on', while 'similar' completes the thought. the guest answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Лучше бы здесь отрастил.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You would have done better to grow it here.' the guest brings out the contrast between 'better' and 'would' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Ой, а вы правда Иосиф Пригожин?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Oh, are you really Iosif Prigozhin?' Vika anchors the line on 'oh', while 'Prigozhin' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "И слава богу!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And thank goodness!' the guest brings out the contrast between 'and' and 'glory' in a few short words. Vika answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Да ладно!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'No way!' Vika anchors the line on 'yes', while 'all right' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Прости, что не поверила.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Forgive me for not believing you.' The grammar packs 'forgive' and 'believed' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "На твоём месте я бы тоже не поверил.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'In your place I would not have believed it either.' Max anchors the line on 'on', while 'to believe' completes the thought. Vika answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "У нас будет девочка.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We are going to have a girl.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'at' before landing on 'girl'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Правда? Девочка?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Really? A girl?' The meaning hinges on 'really' working together with 'girl' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я так и знал!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I knew it all along!' The grammar packs 'I' and 'knew' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
